---
/**
 * Case study: the self-hosted business stack. Deliberately high level (owner decision 2026-10-18):
 * no hostnames, addresses, versions, ports or provider account details.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHead from "@hllmr/brand/components/PageHead.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Self-hosted business stack case study · HLLMR Ventures"
  description="How HLLMR Ventures runs its own mail, files, documents, automation and app hosting on infrastructure it controls, operated with AI agents under written roles and owner approvals."
  sheet="Case study: stack"
  skin="upgrade"
  ogType="article"
>
  <PageHead
    dwg="DWG P-02 · Case study"
    title="A business stack we run ourselves"
    lede="HLLMR runs its mail, files, documents, automation and products on infrastructure it controls, instead of renting each one by the seat. AI agents do much of the operating work. A person approves every change."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap cs">
      <aside class="facts" aria-label="Stack summary">
        <table class="schedule">
          <caption class="anno">Systems schedule</caption>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Mail</th><td>Business mail on our own server, with a backup mail exchanger at the recovery site</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Files</th><td>File sync and online document editing</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Automation</th><td>Workflow automation between services</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Apps</th><td>Silo's API, bot and site; this website</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Recovery</th><td>Encrypted off-host backups, a second site in another country, restore drills</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Watch</th><td>Uptime monitoring and push alerts to the owner</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </aside>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why run it ourselves</h2>
        <p>
          Per-seat cloud suites are convenient until the business wants to know where its data lives, what happens when
          an account is locked, or what the fifth year costs. Running the stack on owned and leased hardware trades that
          convenience for control. The catch is operating work, which used to need a team. That is the work the agents do.
        </p>

        <h2>How it is operated</h2>
        <p>
          Every operating agent has a written role. An infrastructure agent owns the infrastructure code and performs
          changes. An advisory agent reviews plans and reports but changes nothing. Application agents own their own
          services. All of them work in branches and pull requests. Nothing merges, and nothing changes in production,
          until the owner authorizes that specific change in writing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Decisions are kept as numbered decision records on the day they are made, with the options that were turned
          down. Each change to a running service has a request record that says what will change, how it rolls back and
          what was verified afterwards.
        </p>

        <h2>Recovery is proven, not assumed</h2>
        <p>
          A backup that has never been restored is an assumption. Backups are encrypted and stored off the host they
          protect. Restores are drilled into isolated machines and the result is written down, including what the drill
          did not cover. Virtual machines replicate to a recovery site in a second country. Storage is scrubbed on a
          schedule, and a missed scrub raises an alert instead of passing silently. The keys needed to read the backups are
          held in escrow, and the escrow itself is checked.
        </p>

        <h2>Defaults that stay off</h2>
        <p>
          Every system that could send mail or messages is off by default and turned on only by a recorded decision. This
          website sends nothing. A contact link opens your own mail program, and no form on this site stores or forwards
          anything.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Agents can carry real operating load when the work is bounded, recorded and approved.</li>
          <li>Owning the infrastructure is practical for a small business once the operating discipline is written down.</li>
          <li>The same method we would bring to a client's systems, tested on our own first.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Limits, stated plainly</h2>
        <p>
          This is our own infrastructure, described at a high level on purpose; we do not publish its layout. Full
          bare-metal recovery and restoring from outside the estate have not yet been proven end to end, and we say so in
          our own records too.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .cs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.8fr);
    gap: clamp(24px, 5vw, 72px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .facts {
    position: sticky;
    top: 96px;
  }
  .facts th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
    background: var(--raised);
  }
  .facts td {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .prose li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cs {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .facts {
      position: static;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .facts th {
      display: block;
      padding: 12px 12px 0;
      background: transparent;
    }
  }
</style>
